Operator Certification Requirements
Any person operating a water distribution system must hold a valid, unexpired water distribution operator certificate. “Operates a water distribution system” means actions or decisions to control the quality or quantity of drinking water in a water distribution system, including: 1) supervision of other persons operating a water distribution system;
and 2) all activities listed in subdivisions (b) through (d) of section 63770 of the DWOCP Regulations, as an activity that may only be performed by a person with a water

Certification Requirements and Application
To be eligible for certification, an applicant must pass an examination at the applicable grade level. Applicants for an operator certificate at a grade 3 level or above must also meet the applicable minimum experience requirements.
To apply for certification, an applicant must:
• Pass the operator examination for the applicable grade level;
• Within three years of passing the examination, complete and submit the applicable certification form:
o Applicants for an operator certificate at a grade 2 level or below complete and submit the certification request form, provided at the examination testing site with examination results;
o Applicants for an operator certificate at a grade 3 level or above complete and submit the applicable certification application;
• Submit the applicable fee, which is listed on the certification form; and • If applying for a water distribution operator certificate or water treatment operator certificate at the grade 3 level or above, submit the following required documents to demonstrate that the applicant has met the minimum experience requirements:
o Supervisor letter;3
o Organization Chart;4

Certificates are valid for only three years. It is the operator’s responsibility to renew their certificate before it expires regardless of whether the operator received a notice. To assist operators and as a courtesy, the Office of Operator Certification generally sends:
1) a renewal reminder notice approximately 6 months prior to the certificate’s expiration;
and 2) a notice of expiration if the certificate is not renewed before it expires. For more information on these provisions, refer to sections 63840 and 63845 of the DWOCP Regulations. Operators cannot legally operate with an expired certificate. Any person operating a water distribution system, water treatment plant, or water recycling treatment plant without an appropriate, valid, unexpired certification is guilty of a misdemeanor and may be liable civilly.
To be eligible for certificate renewal, an operator must complete the applicable number of continuing education contact hours, on or after the date of issuance of the certificate and before the certificate expires.
To apply for renewal, an applicant must:
• Complete and submit the applicable renewal application;

The State Water Board may grant certification through reciprocity.
To be eligible for certification through reciprocity, an applicant must hold a valid, unexpired, comparable water distribution operator certificate or water treatment operator certificate issued by another State, the United States, a territory, or tribal government that has been designated as the primacy agency by the United States Environmental Protection Agency, or a unit of any of these. An applicant’s education and experience must be equitable to the California requirements.
To apply for reciprocity an applicant must:
• Complete and submit the applicable reciprocity application;
• Submit the applicable fee, which is listed on the reciprocity application; and • Submit the following required documents:
o Copy of a valid, unexpired, operator certificate;

