Remedial Optimization
Chapter 19: Remedial Optimization
Long-Term Groundwater Monitoring
December 2015
This section focuses on optimizing long-term groundwater monitoring programs and discusses the following long-term monitoring optimization (LTMO) items:
• Long-term groundwater monitoring program (LTM) and the need to document the LTM in the LUFT site’s groundwater monitoring plan (GMP);
• Primary objective and overall objectives of the LTMO;
• What are the key elements of the LTM targeted for a detailed LTMO;
• Qualitative and quantitative evaluation methods of LTMO;
• Cost feasibility of implementing LTMO study and recommendations at LUFT sites with limited size monitoring programs.
Long-term monitoring program (LTM) refers to the groundwater monitoring conducted at a LUFT site after an active, passive, or containment remedy has been selected and installed. The primary purpose of LTM is remedial performance monitoring in addition to other regulatory mandated objectives.

Note: The main objective of conducting LTMO is to reduce the cost of monitoring without losing program effectiveness.
Historical experience of conducting monitoring optimization at the CERCLA/RCRA sites including the Naval/Air Force sites have resulted in net cost reduction in monitoring at these sites. It appears that the number of monitoring points (wells) ranged from the tens to the hundreds at these large sites. The size of the monitoring program at the UST Cleanup Fund’s LUFT sites in California is comparably much smaller. There is the concern that in general the cost of the optimization study and its implementation at LUFT sites may outweigh the benefits and result in no net reduction in monitoring costs. Ensuring that the “right” candidate LUFT site is selected and a “low cost” optimization approach is utilized may alleviate this concern.
Elements of the LTM program targeted for optimization evaluation include the following:
• The number of monitoring points (wells);
• The frequency and duration of monitoring;
• The analyte list and quality assurance/quality control (QA/QC) samples;
• The data evaluation, management, and reporting procedures.

