after being brought to the entity’s attention or reoccurring, or both;
b. Whether the entity has a history of noncompliance; and, c. Good-faith efforts to eliminate noncompliance.
7. Evidence of, or threat of, pollution or nuisance caused by violations;
8. The magnitude of impacts of the violation(s);
9. Case-by-case factors that may mitigate a violation;
10. Impact or threat to high priority watersheds or water bodies (e.g., due to the vulnerability of an existing beneficial use or an existing state of impairment);
11. Potential to abate effects of the violations;
12. Strength of evidence in the record to support the enforcement action;
13. Availability of resources for enforcement; and, 14. Whether the action is likely to encourage similarly situated members of the regulated public to voluntarily identify, and avoid or correct similar violations.
